<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Lament · c. 1878 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Aloha ʻOe</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A farewell song written in about 1878 by Liliʻuokalani, who later became Queen of Hawaii.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Liliʻuokalani</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Liliʻuokalani</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Native Hawaiians; the Hawaiian Kingdom</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hawaiian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Maunawili, Oʻahu, Hawaiian Kingdom (present-day Hawaii, United States)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>c. 1878 (her manuscript is dated 1878; the ride that inspired it 1877 or 1878)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Liliʻuokalani (1878; she died in 1917))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aloha-oe">Aloha ʻOe</a>&quot; (&quot;Farewell to Thee&quot;) was written about 1878 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a>, then a princess of the Hawaiian Kingdom. The accounts of its origin differ in detail, but all agree that it was inspired by a farewell embrace given by Colonel James Harbottle Boyd at the end of a horseback trip that the princess and her party made in 1877 or 1878 to the Boyd ranch at Maunawili, on the windward side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>. The embrace was given either to her sister, Princess Likelike Cleghorn, or to a young woman at the ranch. On the ride back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> the princess began to hum a tune; Charles Wilson remarked that it sounded like &quot;The Lone Rock by the Sea&quot;, and the others took up the humming when the party rested in an orange grove on the Honolulu side of the Pali. The song was finished later at Washington Place.</p><p>The Hawaiʻi State Archives holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a>'s handwritten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, dated 1878, with the score, the Hawaiian words and her own English translation. A note in her hand, apparently added later, records that it was composed at Maunawili in 1878 and that the Royal Hawaiian Band played it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> in August 1883, where it became very popular. Parts of the melody resemble &quot;The Rock Beside the Sea&quot;, published by Charles Crozat Converse in 1857 and itself drawn from the Croatian and Serbian folk song &quot;Sedi Mara na kamen studencu&quot;, and the chorus of George Frederick Root's &quot;There's Music in the Air&quot; (1854).</p><p>The first known recording was issued by Berliner Gramophone in 1898; Columbia recorded Nani Alapai and Henry N. Clark singing it as a duet in 1911, and the 1913 recording by the Hawaiian Quintette, with Walter Kolomoku on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> guitar, was chosen for the National Recording Registry of the Library of Congress in 2025. It became her best-known song and a common cultural symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, recorded by Bing Crosby (1936), Elvis Presley for the film Blue Hawaii (1961), Don Ho (1970) and Johnny Cash, among many others. The orchestra played it at the closing ceremony of the 1932 Summer Olympics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> as the flame went out. In October 1997 the Chinese president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-zemin">Jiang Zemin</a>, arriving in Hawaii on a state visit, played it on a Hawaiian lap steel guitar at a dinner with Governor Ben Cayetano, recalling that he had often played it as a student in the 1940s. Since 1926 the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> has held a farewell ceremony for graduating seniors under the same name.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of parting between loved ones, born of a farewell embrace. Its mood is tender and wistful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hawaiian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hawaiian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Haʻaheo e ka ua i nā pali</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Proud is the rain on the cliffs</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Ke nihi aʻela i ka nahele</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Creeping stealthily through the forest</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">E hahai (uhai) ana paha i ka liko</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Following (pursuing) perhaps the leaf bud</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Pua ʻāhihi lehua o uka</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The ʻāhihi lehua flower of the uplands</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Aloha ʻoe, aloha ʻoe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Farewell to you, farewell to you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">E ke onaona noho i ka lipo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O fragrant one who dwells in the deep dark blue [?]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">One fond embrace,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One fond embrace,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">A hoʻi aʻe au</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And then I go [?]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Until we meet again</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Until we meet again</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">ʻO ka haliʻa aloha i hiki mai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The fond remembrance that has come</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Ke hone aʻe nei i</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is sweetly stirring my</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kuʻu manawa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Heart [?]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">ʻO ʻoe nō kuʻu ipo aloha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You indeed are my beloved sweetheart</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">A loko e hana nei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Within (me), at work now [?]</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Maopopo kuʻu ʻike i ka nani</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I know clearly the beauty</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Nā pua rose o Maunawili</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The rose flowers of Maunawili</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">I laila hiaʻai nā manu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There the birds delight</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Mikiʻala i ka nani o ka liko</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Alert to the beauty of the leaf bud</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Liliʻuokalani (1878); the Hawaiian text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aloha_%CA%BBOe" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>lines 2.3 and 3.1: 'One fond embrace,' and 'Until we meet again' are in English in the original (Liliʻuokalani's own words); kept as they are.</li><li>line 1.3: the source gives the variant '(uhai)' beside 'hahai'; both mean to follow, chase, pursue. Kept as one line.</li><li>line 1.4: ʻāhihi is a kind of lehua (ʻōhiʻa) blossom; 'o uka' is 'of the uplands, inland'.</li><li>line 2.2: 'lipo' is the deep blue-black of depths or of distant forest; 'noho i ka lipo' can be 'dwelling in the dim (forest) depths'. 'Onaona' is fragrant, also gentle, sweet.</li><li>line 2.4: 'A hoʻi aʻe au' is literally 'and I return (go back)': taken as 'before I go'.</li><li>line 3.4: 'manawa' is time, and also the seat of the feelings (literally the fontanelle); here 'my heart, my feelings', though 'my time' is a possible reading.</li><li>line 4.2: 'A loko e hana nei' is elliptic: 'within, working now', i.e. stirring inside me. Sense uncertain.</li><li>Maunawili: a place on windward Oʻahu, kept as a name.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="vjC15aK53ig" data-title="Aloha ʻOe: Tia Carrere and Daniel Ho"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Tia Carrere and Daniel Ho</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Daniel Ho (Daniel Ho Creations). Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vjC15aK53ig" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Aloha Oe (Jack London, short story)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">features the chorus of the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hula Lou</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the chorus melody used as counterpoint in the chorus</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Aloha ʻOe farewell ceremony, University of Tennessee</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1926</span><span class="sg-chain-h">named after it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Recording by Bing Crosby</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1936</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Blue Hawaii (film, sung by Elvis Presley)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording for the soundtrack</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lilo &amp; Stitch (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2002</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung by Nani as a farewell to her sister</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Train to Busan (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">partly sung; instrumental at the conclusion</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">American VI: Ain't No Grave (Johnny Cash)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2010</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording (made 2003)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Warner Bros. Looney Tunes and Merrie Melodies (Carl Stalling)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">stock musical cue for Hawaiian scenes</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Rock Beside the Sea (Charles Crozat Converse)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1857</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody resembles it in part</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sedi Mara na kamen studencu</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Croatian/Serbian folk song behind Converse's</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">There's Music in the Air (George Frederick Root)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1854</span><span class="sg-chain-h">resembles its chorus</span></li></ul></section>
